ich are excluded from generation. The PRD might have separate requirements for both the magefiles directory and the cmd/version package.\n\nI'm thinking about whether to reference the test suite as well, since there's likely a test specification document that would apply here.\n\nActually, I realize the test suite might cover multiple use cases or might not exist yet for this release, so I should mention it conditionally in the required reading rather than assume it exists.\n\nNow I'm verifying that the task description is truly self-contained—all the necessary files are referenced, the requirements are explicit, and the patterns are clear enough for an agent to execute independently within the project's standard conventions.\n\nLet me write out the final response now. response text:\n\n---\n\n## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** go-unix-utils is a specification-driven experiment that derives formal specs from C source code of classic Unix utilities, generates Go implementations via AI (mage-claude-orchestrator), and verifies parity through differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify) with shared infrastructure in pkg/ (testutils, sys, format) and per-utility implementations in cmd/. Specifications live in docs/.\n\n**Implementation state:** Code exists for pkg/testutils, pkg/sys, pkg/format, cmd/cat, cmd/du, cmd/sponge, and cmd/wc (confirmed by source code). Specs are complete for these plus cmd/ls. Releases 02.0-02.2 (trivial utilities, tee, head, seq) have pending specs and cannot proceed to code generation.\n\n**Current release:** rel00.0 (foundational infrastructure) has one remaining code generation target: cmd/version (per DD7, the only generation target in prd011-magefiles). All other rel00.0 use cases (format, sys, testutils) have code.\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\nrel00.0 is the earliest incomplete release with uc002-format, uc003-sys, and uc004-testutils already implemented, while uc001-magefiles is spec-complete but hasn't started code generation. rel01.1-01.3 and rel03.0 have working implementations for cat, sponge, du, and wc. rel02.0-02.2 is blocked on pending specs since the pipeline reads from docs/ but doesn't write there yet.\n\nI'm checking whether cmd/version needs a test suite—the metadata shows testdir is empty and testfiles is zero, which makes sense since cmd/version doesn't require differential testing. P2 specifies splitting implementation and tests, but if no tests are defined in the spec, I should probably just implement the command itself without creating test files.\n\nLooking at the file constraint, \"5-7 files\" is a maximum, not a minimum, so proposing just cmd/version/main.go is fine for something this straightforward. I need to verify the task fits within the Claude timeout before finalizing.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":72052,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":72052},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6fa1aec7-3f0c-4110-b038-0e9a53ef21d3","uuid":"b2bae479-c237-4b9e-a703-b017a8540737"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HJ5PSPVborGCfAdgcVuGCo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** go-unix-utils derives formal specifications from C source code of classic Unix utilities, generates Go implementations via AI (mage-claude-orchestrator), and verifies parity through differential testing against GNU reference binaries. The goal is to characterize where the methodology holds and where it breaks down.\n\n**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Shared infrastructure in `pkg/` (testutils, sys, format), per-utility implementations in `cmd/`, specifications in `docs/`. Human operator writes specs; orchestrator generates code. Generation targets only `cmd/` and `pkg/`.\n\n**Implementation state (from source code):** Code exists for `pkg/testutils`, `pkg/sys`, `pkg/format`, `cmd/cat`, `cmd/du`, `cmd/sponge`, and `cmd/wc`. Specs are complete for these plus `cmd/ls` (core and extended). Releases 02.0–02.2 (true, false, yes, basename, dirname, tee, head, seq) have pending specs — the pipeline cannot generate code for them since it reads but does not write `docs/` (DD7).\n\n**Current release:** rel00.0 (foundational infrastructure) has one remaining code generation target: `cmd/version` (per DD7, the only generation target in prd011-magefiles; `magefiles/` is excluded). All other rel00.0 use cases (format, sys, testutils) have code confirmed in source.\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\n1. **rel00.0** — earliest incomplete release. uc002–uc004 have code (source confirmed). uc001-magefiles is spec_complete but `cmd/version` code not started. This is the highest-priority actionable target.\n2. **rel01.1–01.3, rel03.0** — code exists for cat, sponge, du, wc. Complete.\n3. **rel02.0–02.2** — specs pending. Pipeline cannot write specs (DD5, DD7). Blocked.\n4. **rel04.0–04.1** — ls specs complete, code not started. Actionable but lower priority than rel00.0.\n\n**Decision:** Propose `cmd/version` for rel00.0-uc001-magefiles — the earliest actionable code generation target.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"cmd/version: implement version reporting command (prd011-magefiles, rel00.0-uc001)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd011-magefiles.yaml (PRD for cmd/version -- read this first to identify specific requirement IDs; cmd/version is the only code generation target in this PRD per DD7; magefiles/ requirements are excluded from generation)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel00.0-uc001-magefiles.yaml (use case covering magefiles and cmd/version)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (cmd/ component description, DD7 generation scope exclusions for prd011-magefiles)\n      - cmd/cat/main.go (pattern for SIGPIPE handler registration, flag parsing, exit code handling)\n      - cmd/wc/main.go (pattern for buffered stdout and write error detection)\n      - go.mod (module path for import statements)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/version/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"Version command: prints version info to stdout, supports build-time injection via ldflags\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Print version information to stdout as a single line containing the program name and version string. The exact content and format are defined in prd011-magefiles -- read the PRD and implement its version output requirements.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Support build-time version injection via Go ldflags. Declare a package-level var version string that can be set with go build -ldflags \\\"-X main.version=v1.0.0\\\". Default to \\\"dev\\\" when not set at build time.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Handle SIGPIPE by exiting cleanly with code 0. Register the signal handler before producing any output, following the pattern from cmd/cat/main.go (lines 47-52) and cmd/wc/main.go (lines 169-174).\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Exit 0 on successful version output. If writing to stdout fails (e.g., broken pipe not caught by SIGPIPE handler), exit 1.\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Include copyright header (\\\"Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\\\"), SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT, and a package comment referencing prd011-magefiles with the specific requirement IDs implemented.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Declare var version = \\\"dev\\\" at package level. Build tooling sets this via -ldflags \\\"-X main.version=...\\\". This is standard Go practice for compile-time version embedding and is how the mage build target will inject the version.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Follow the minimal main() skeleton from existing cmd/ packages: (1) register SIGPIPE handler via signal.Notify, (2) produce output, (3) os.Exit with appropriate code. No flag parsing is needed unless the PRD requires flags beyond the default flag.Parse() call.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Use fmt.Fprintln(os.Stdout, ...) for version output. A buffered writer is unnecessary for single-line output -- unlike cmd/wc which produces multiple lines, cmd/version emits one line and exits.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/version/main.go compiles: go build ./cmd/version/ succeeds with no errors.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Running the compiled binary prints version information to stdout and exits with code 0.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"When built without ldflags, the binary prints a default version string containing \\\"dev\\\".\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"When built with -ldflags \\\"-X main.version=v1.0.0\\\", the binary output contains \\\"v1.0.0\\\".\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"The file includes the copyright header, SPDX license identifier, and package comment with PRD reference, matching the conventions in cmd/cat/main.go lines 1-7.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":72052,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":72052},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6fa1aec7-3f0c-4110-b038-0e9a53ef21d3","uuid":"2bae46f3-6cd4-4e0d-8cf2-fd8908753093"}
